// NEGATIVE TEST -- a macro body must write `$x`, not `x` (N3-12).
//
// THIS ONE IS THE HYGIENE DEFECT WEARING A DIFFERENT HAT, and it is why the
// refusal is worth its weight. Substitution keys on `$name`. A bare `x` in the
// body of `macro double!(x)` is therefore NOT the parameter: it is a free
// identifier, and expansion is textual, so it resolves against whatever the
// CALL SITE has in scope.
//
// Measured, compiling and running with no diagnostic:
//     macro double!(x) { x * 2 }
//     fn main() { let x = 3; print_int(double!(21)); }
// printed 6. The argument 21 was discarded and the caller's own `x` was
// doubled. With no `x` at the call site the same program failed with
// "Undefined variable or function: 'x'", so whether this defect is loud or
// silent depended on a variable name in a different file.
//
// Refused where the BODY is written, which is the line that is wrong. What is
// NOT refused, and cannot be while expansion is textual, is a body reading a
// caller name that is not a parameter -- that is N3-13, and it is measured and
// reported rather than defended.
macro double!(x) { x * 2 }

fn main() {
    let x = 3;
    print_int(double!(21));
}
